# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: clautolisp-error-silent-under-autocad: ERROR runs without a vendor-operator notice under --dialect autocad
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: master
#+DATE: 2026-10-15

* Symptom

AutoCAD has no =error= function
(=issues/closed/alfe-autocad-error-primitive-masks-load-failure.issue=), yet

#+begin_example
clautolisp-sbcl --no-init -q --dialect autocad -x '(error "x")'
#+end_example

ran clautolisp's =ERROR= builtin with no =[vendor-operator]= notice, and
=(type error)= is =SUBR=. Found while making alfe's runtime raise carry its
message (2.3.20).

* Cause

The vendor-only operator table (=autolisp-runtime/source/operator-availability.lisp=)
is generated from the spec's Availability sections, and the autolisp-spec had
NO Function Entry for =error= at all -- so =ERROR= had no owner and passed as
portable.

* Evidence -- AutoCAD settled, BricsCAD NOT

- AutoCAD 2022 / Windows: no ERROR (SCHME+ pipeline 86651, "no function
  definition: ERROR"; encoding probe =error-primitive.bound = no=, jobs
  16776117747 and 17033123931).
- BricsCAD V25 / Windows (job 17033123930) and V26 / macOS (job 17034595786):
  the encoding probe says =error-primitive.bound = yes= and
  =error-primitive.call = raised: error=. This first read as "BricsCAD has
  ERROR" and was briefly recorded so; it does not hold. =.bound= tests
  ATOMS-FAMILY, which may list a merely interned symbol (the probe source
  itself reads =error=), and BricsCAD reports the bare word "error" for an
  UNDEFINED function too (alfe-cad-load-error-message-says-only-error).
  The parent session (pjb asked whether ERROR is just a clautolisp
  extension) pointed this out.

* Resolved -- clautolisp 2.3.21 / alfe 2.3.21, spec 0.14.2

- autolisp-spec: new =** Function Entry: ERROR= (chapter 15) with the
  observations above, an Availability that credits NO vendor, and a
  =*** clautolisp= note. The generated vendor table is unchanged.
- clautolisp owns it until the probe answers: =*clautolisp-only-operators*=
  (autolisp-runtime api.lisp, a =SPEC-UNCERTAIN= marker) gives the ERROR subr
  the owner =:clautolisp=, and the existing =[vendor-operator]= machinery does
  the rest: one notice per session, =ERROR is a clautolisp-only operator=,
  under =--strict=, =--autocad= and =--bricscad= (any spelling); silent under
  =--clautolisp= and =--lax=; the call proceeds everywhere. *pjb to confirm
  this classification.*
- New probe suite =probes/sources/probe-error-function.lsp= (in the
  manifest): =(type error)=, atoms-family for comparison, and -- only where
  ERROR is a function -- the catch-all message of =(error "x")=, of a symbol
  argument, of no argument, next to that of an undefined function. Open
  questions in =issues/open/deferred-spec-research.issue=, ERROR. If BricsCAD
  has ERROR, credit BricsCAD in the spec, regenerate, and remove ERROR from
  =*clautolisp-only-operators*=.
- alfe: =autolisp-force-error= (bootstrap) calls =error= when it is a SUBR,
  which on a hosted clautolisp engine (strict by default) would now have shown
  the notice for the RUNTIME's raise. It binds =*AUTOLISP-WARN-OUT-OF-DIALECT*=
  as a nil local, which silences that call only; a later user call of =error=
  still warns. On the vendor hosts the local is inert.
- Tests: =vendor-operator-error-is-clautolisp-only=,
  =vendor-operator-error-warns-out-of-clautolisp=,
  =vendor-operator-error-is-silent-under-clautolisp-and-lax= (builtins-core),
  and =protocol-runtime-raise-carries-its-message-to-user-handlers= now
  requires no =[vendor-operator]= on the engine's stderr.
